    "SKILL.md": "---\nname: self-skill-learner\ndescription: Learn and improve skills autonomously through iterative evaluation loops, reflection, and targeted rewrites. Use whenever the user wants a skill to get better over time with measurable progress.\nlicense: Complete terms in LICENSE.txt\n---\n\n# Self-Skill Learner\n\nA skill for improving other skills (including itself) using repeatable feedback loops.\n\n## Mission\n\nTurn weak or inconsistent skills into reliable, reusable skills by iterating on:\n- trigger quality,\n- instruction clarity,\n- output quality,\n- and success rate across realistic prompts.\n\n## Core Loop\n\nRun this loop until stop criteria are met:\n\n1. **Assess**\n   - Read the current skill frontmatter and body.\n   - Identify likely failure modes (under-triggering, ambiguity, over-constraint, missing guardrails).\n   - Define measurable targets for this iteration.\n\n2. **Hypothesize**\n   - Propose the smallest high-impact change.\n   - Explain why that specific change should improve behavior.\n\n3. **Edit**\n   - Update the skill with focused modifications.\n   - Keep instructions concise and generalizable.\n   - Preserve the skill's name unless the user explicitly asks to rename.\n\n4. **Evaluate**\n   - Test with realistic prompts that represent real user intent.\n   - Include edge cases and near-miss prompts.\n   - Compare outcomes against the prior version.\n\n5. **Learn**\n   - Record what improved, what regressed, and what is still uncertain.\n   - Keep lessons that generalize; discard one-off noise.\n\n6. **Plan Next Iteration**\n   - Choose one next improvement with the best expected payoff.\n\n## What To Improve\n\n### 1) Triggering Quality\n- Make description explicit about when to use the skill.\n- Include multiple natural-language trigger cues.\n- Avoid vague wording that causes under-triggering.\n\n### 2) Instruction Quality\n- Remove duplicated or conflicting rules.\n- Prefer clear rationale over rigid, brittle prescriptions.\n- Ensure steps are actionable and ordered.\n\n### 3) Output Quality\n- Define expected output structure when useful.\n- Ensure outputs are useful to the user's likely audience.\n- Emphasize factual grounding and explicit uncertainty.\n\n### 4) Reliability\n- Stress test with varied prompt styles (short, long, messy, casual).\n- Ensure behavior remains stable across similar prompts.\n- Reduce reliance on fragile wording hacks.\n\n## Evaluation Framework\n\nFor each iteration, track:\n- **pass_rate**: fraction of prompts meeting expectations.\n- **trigger_precision**: how often the skill triggers when it should.\n- **trigger_recall**: how often it fails to trigger when needed.\n- **quality_notes**: concise qualitative observations.\n\nUse realistic eval prompts:\n- 8-12 should-trigger prompts.\n- 8-12 should-not-trigger near-miss prompts.\n- Prefer tricky boundary cases over obvious ones.\n\n## Iteration Output Template\n\nFor every cycle, report:\n1. **Hypothesis**\n2. **Change Made**\n3. **Evidence**\n4. **Learning**\n5. **Next Step**\n\n## Stop Criteria\n\nStop when one of these is true:\n- targets are met for two consecutive iterations,\n- improvements become marginal,\n- or user approves final version.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Do not fabricate evaluation outcomes.\n- Do not claim improvements without evidence.\n- Do not optimize only for benchmark prompts (avoid overfitting).\n- Do not broaden triggers so far that unrelated tasks invoke the skill.\n\n## Trigger Examples\n\nThis skill should trigger for prompts like:\n- \\\"Improve this skill so it triggers more reliably.\\\"\n- \\\"Run an iteration loop to make this skill better.\\\"\n- \\\"Analyze why this skill underperforms and rewrite it.\\\"\n- \\\"Tune the description and instructions based on eval results.\\\""
